---
import Base from '../../../layouts/Base.astro';
const body = "<section class=\"hero wrap\"> <div class=\"shead reveal\"> <div class=\"label\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">// Services</span><span class=\"folio\">01</span></div> </div> <h1 class=\"h1 reveal d1\" style=\"max-width:22ch;margin-top:18px;font-size:clamp(32px,4.4vw,60px)\">E-Commerce Development — Built to Be Run, Not Just Launched</h1> <p class=\"lead reveal d2\" style=\"margin-top:22px\">An online store that still makes sense when your catalogue grows from twenty products to two hundred.</p> <div class=\"reveal d3\" style=\"margin-top:32px;display:flex;gap:14px;flex-wrap:wrap\"> <a href=\"https://wa.me/6285927277560?text=Hi!%20I'd%20like%20to%20talk%20about%20an%20e-commerce%20project...\" class=\"btn\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-wa=\"hero\" data-page=\"/en/services/ecommerce-website.html\"> Free Consultation<span class=\"arr\">↗</span> </a> <a href=\"/en/pricing\" class=\"btn ghost\">See pricing <span class=\"arr\">↗</span></a> </div> </section> <hr class=\"hair\" style=\"margin:0 var(--gut)\"> <section class=\"pad-sm wrap\"> <div class=\"shead reveal\"> <div class=\"label\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">// 02 — The problem</span><span class=\"folio\">02</span></div> </div> <div class=\"persp\"> <div class=\"reveal\"><p class=\"big\">Online stores fail after launch, not at launch.</p></div> <div class=\"reveal d1\"> <p class=\"lead\" style=\"margin-bottom:20px\">Almost everyone asks about the catalogue layout and the payment gateway. Almost nobody asks who updates stock every day, what happens when a buyer wants to cancel, or what it feels like to add fifty new products through the dashboard.</p><p class=\"lead\" style=\"margin-bottom:20px\">That is what decides whether a store lives. A site can look perfect on launch day and be abandoned three months later — not because there were no buyers, but because running it was painful enough that nobody wanted to.</p><p class=\"lead\" style=\"margin-bottom:20px\">So I start from the unglamorous end: who operates this, how often, and with how much time. The storefront gets built once that is clear. A neglected store loses to a mediocre but maintained one, every time.</p> </div> </div> </section> <hr class=\"hair\" style=\"margin:0 var(--gut)\"> <section class=\"pad-sm wrap\"> <div class=\"shead reveal\"> <div class=\"label\"> <div><span class=\"eyebrow\">// 03 — What's included</span><h2 class=\"h2\" style=\"margin-top:18px;max-width:20ch\">What you actually get.</h2></div> <span class=\"folio\">03</span> </div> </div> <div class=\"deflist reveal\"> <div><b>Catalogue architecture</b><p>Category, variant, and attribute structure that survives growth. The most expensive thing to fix later.</p></div><div><b>Product pages</b><p>Layouts that answer buyer objections before they close the tab.</p></div><div><b>Cart &amp; checkout</b><p>The shortest viable path. Every extra field is a lost buyer.</p></div><div><b>Payments</b><p>Gateway integration matched to how your buyers actually pay.</p></div><div><b>Shipping</b><p>Rates and carrier options that do not ambush people at the last step.</p></div><div><b>Admin dashboard</b><p>Tested against real tasks: add a product, change stock, process an order, run a promotion.</p></div><div><b>Product SEO</b><p>URL structure, product schema, and meta correct from the start.</p></div> </div> </section> <section class=\"pad-sm wrap\"> <div class=\"shead reveal\"> <div class=\"label\"> <div> <span class=\"eyebrow\">// 04 — Fit</span> <h2 class=\"h2\" style=\"margin-top:18px;max-width:22ch\">Who this is for — and who it isn't.</h2> <p class=\"lead\" style=\"margin-top:16px;max-width:62ch\">The right column matters as much as the left. I would rather lose the project here than have us both discover it in week five.</p> </div> <span class=\"folio\">04</span> </div> </div> <div class=\"fit-grid reveal\"> <div class=\"fit yes\"><b>A good fit if</b><ul><li>Businesses already selling somewhere else that want their own channel.</li><li>Brands whose catalogue will keep growing and needs a structure that holds.</li><li>Sellers tired of handing margin and customer data to a marketplace.</li><li>Teams with someone dedicated to stock and orders.</li></ul></div> <div class=\"fit no\"><b>Not a fit if</b><ul><li>You have never sold this product at all. Validate somewhere cheaper first.</li><li>Nobody will run the store daily. This is ongoing work, not a one-off project.</li><li>You want to replicate a major marketplace. You do not have their hundred-person team, and your buyers are not asking for it.</li><li>You have no product photography. In e-commerce, photos are not a garnish — they are the product.</li></ul></div> </div> </section> <hr class=\"hair\" style=\"margin:0 var(--gut)\"> <section class=\"pad-sm wrap\"> <div class=\"shead reveal\"> <div class=\"label\"> <div> <span class=\"eyebrow\">// 05 — Process</span> <h2 class=\"h2\" style=\"margin-top:18px\">How this runs.</h2> <p class=\"lead\" style=\"margin-top:16px;max-width:62ch\">4–8 weeks. Catalogue size and integration count are the two biggest variables.</p> </div> <span class=\"folio\">05</span> </div> </div> <div class=\"proc\"> <div class=\"proc-step reveal\"><div class=\"pn\">1.</div><h3>Operational discovery</h3><p>Not just the products — who runs this, how often, with what tools. That shapes the system.</p></div><div class=\"proc-step reveal\"><div class=\"pn\">2.</div><h3>Catalogue architecture</h3><p>Categories, variants, and attributes agreed before pages are built. Getting this wrong means an expensive data migration in six months.</p></div><div class=\"proc-step reveal\"><div class=\"pn\">3.</div><h3>Design &amp; build</h3><p>Built and tested with real product data, not lorem ipsum.</p></div><div class=\"proc-step reveal\"><div class=\"pn\">4.</div><h3>Transaction testing &amp; launch</h3><p>Real transactions tested end to end before go-live: payment, notifications, shipping, cancellation.</p></div> </div> </section> <section class=\"pad-sm wrap\"> <div class=\"shead reveal\"> <div class=\"label\"> <div> <span class=\"eyebrow\">// 06 — Pricing</span> <h2 class=\"h2\" style=\"margin-top:18px\">From IDR 15,000,000 (approx. USD 950)</h2> <p class=\"lead\" style=\"margin-top:16px;max-width:62ch\">Indicative conversion only; quoted in IDR. Depends on catalogue size, integrations, and how complex the pricing and shipping rules are.</p> <div style=\"margin-top:26px\"><a href=\"/en/pricing\" class=\"btn ghost\">Full pricing &amp; what's excluded <span class=\"arr\">↗</span></a></div> </div> <span class=\"folio\">06</span> </div> </div> </section> <section class=\"pad-sm wrap\"> <div class=\"shead reveal\"> <div class=\"label\"> <div><span class=\"eyebrow\">// 07 — Examples</span><h2 class=\"h2\" style=\"margin-top:18px\">Comparable work.</h2></div> <span class=\"folio\">07</span> </div> </div> <div class=\"more-grid reveal\"> <a class=\"more-card\" href=\"https://farmfreshbites.wixstudio.com/farm-fresh-bites\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"> <div class=\"mthumb\"><img src=\"/img/work/farm-fresh-bites.webp\" alt=\"Screenshot of the Farm Fresh Bites project\" width=\"1145\" height=\"750\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></div> <div class=\"mt\"><h3 class=\"h3\">Farm Fresh Bites</h3><span class=\"mtag\">.Wix Studio .eCommerce</span></div> <p>A hyperlocal business in Mumbai delivering responsibly sourced exotic fruit. The challenge: sell a premium product while keeping a real social mission visible. I built a Wix Studio storefront that does both without either undercutting the other.</p> <span class=\"open\">View site ↗</span> </a><a class=\"more-card\" href=\"https://rasa-lokal-sentuh-asia.lovable.app/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"> <div class=\"mthumb\"><img src=\"/img/work/rasalokal.webp\" alt=\"Screenshot of the Rasalokal project\" width=\"1200\" height=\"575\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></div> <div class=\"mt\"><h3 class=\"h3\">Rasalokal</h3><span class=\"mtag\">.Creator .Lovable</span></div> <p>A platform connecting small-batch Indonesian snack producers to modern distribution and marketplaces. The classic trap in this category: the &quot;from a home kitchen&quot; story reads sentimental until the product itself stops looking serious. I split the two audiences that had been colliding — retail buyers and B2B buyers — starting at the navigation.</p> <span class=\"open\">View site ↗</span> </a> </div> <p class=\"lead\" style=\"margin-top:24px;font-size:15px\"> <a href=\"/en/work\" style=\"text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:4px\">See the full portfolio</a> — with the reasoning behind each decision.\n</p> </section> <hr class=\"hair\" style=\"margin:0 var(--gut)\"> <section class=\"pad-sm wrap\"> <div class=\"shead reveal\"> <div class=\"label\"> <div><span class=\"eyebrow\">// 08 — Questions</span><h2 class=\"h2\" style=\"margin-top:18px\">What people usually ask.</h2></div> <span class=\"folio\">08</span> </div> </div> <div class=\"faq reveal\"> <details> <summary><span class=\"fq\">/01</span>Why not just use a marketplace?</summary> <p class=\"fa\">For many businesses a marketplace is the right answer, and I will say so if it is. It gives you traffic you do not have to build. Your own store gives you margin, customer data, and control of the experience. Most healthy brands use both. If all your sales come from a marketplace today and you have no audience of your own, a store may not be your priority yet.</p> </details><details> <summary><span class=\"fq\">/02</span>Which platform do you use?</summary> <p class=\"fa\">Usually WordPress with WooCommerce, or Wix Studio for simpler catalogues. Both are things you can manage yourself and neither locks you in. The choice comes after I understand your catalogue scale and who maintains it.</p> </details><details> <summary><span class=\"fq\">/03</span>Can you integrate our existing inventory system?</summary> <p class=\"fa\">Often yes, but it depends on what you use and whether it has an open API. That is one of the first things I check in discovery, because an impossible integration is much better discovered in week one than in week six.</p> </details> </div> </section> <section class=\"cta-ink pad\"> <img class=\"bgimg\" src=\"/img/cta-bg.webp\" width=\"1440\" height=\"1024\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" alt=\"\" aria-hidden=\"true\"> <div class=\"wrap\" style=\"position:relative\"> <span class=\"eyebrow reveal\">// Let's talk</span> <h2 class=\"h2 reveal d1\" style=\"margin:24px 0 36px;max-width:20ch\">Sounds like a fit? Let's test that first.</h2> <div class=\"reveal d2\"> <a href=\"https://wa.me/6285927277560?text=Hi!%20I'd%20like%20to%20talk%20about%20an%20e-commerce%20project...\" class=\"btn on-ink\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" data-wa=\"cta\" data-page=\"/en/services/ecommerce-website.html\"> Free Consultation<span class=\"arr\">↗</span> </a> </div> </div> </section>";
const extraSchema = [{"@type":"Service","name":"ecommerce website development","description":"E-commerce builds designed to be run, not just launched. Catalogue architecture first, storefront second.","url":"https://mglportfolio.vercel.app/en/services/ecommerce-website","serviceType":"E-Commerce Website","provider":{"@id":"https://mglportfolio.vercel.app/#service"},"areaServed":{"@type":"Country","name":"Indonesia"},"inLanguage":"en-US"},{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Home","item":"https://mglportfolio.vercel.app/en"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Services","item":"https://mglportfolio.vercel.app/en/services"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"E-Commerce Website","item":"https://mglportfolio.vercel.app/en/services/ecommerce-website"}]},{"@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"Why not just use a marketplace?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"For many businesses a marketplace is the right answer, and I will say so if it is. It gives you traffic you do not have to build. Your own store gives you margin, customer data, and control of the experience. Most healthy brands use both. If all your sales come from a marketplace today and you have no audience of your own, a store may not be your priority yet."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Which platform do you use?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Usually WordPress with WooCommerce, or Wix Studio for simpler catalogues. Both are things you can manage yourself and neither locks you in. The choice comes after I understand your catalogue scale and who maintains it."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can you integrate our existing inventory system?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Often yes, but it depends on what you use and whether it has an open API. That is one of the first things I check in discovery, because an impossible integration is much better discovered in week one than in week six."}}]}];
